      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side. We can factor the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\):\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8)\n\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nSo the numerator can be written as:\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the original equation:\n\n\\[\n5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n5 = x(x - 4)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe factor the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0\n\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\n\\[\n5 + (-1) = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
