      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by simplifying the given equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\).\n\nFirst, we perform polynomial long division on the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) by the denominator \\(x + 2\\).\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator \\(x^3\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(x^2\\).\n2. Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\).\n3. Subtract \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\) from \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n4. Divide the leading term \\(-4x^2\\) by \\(x\\) to get \\(-4x\\).\n5. Multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n6. Subtract \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) from \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(0\\).\n\nSo, the result of the division is \\(x^2 - 4x\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the original equation as:\n\\[5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} = \\frac{x(x^2 - 4x)}{x + 2} = \\frac{x^2(x - 4)}{x + 2}.\\]\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by \\(x + 2\\) to clear the denominator:\n\\[5(x + 2) = x^2(x - 4).\\]\nExpanding both sides, we get:\n\\[5x + 10 = x^3 - 4x^2.\\]\nRearranging all terms to one side of the equation, we obtain:\n\\[x^3 - 4x^2 - 5x - 10 = 0.\\]\n\nWe need to find the roots of the polynomial equation \\(x^3 - 4x^2 - 5x - 10 = 0\\). We can use the Rational Root Theorem to test possible rational roots, which are the factors of \\(-10\\) (the constant term) divided by the factors of \\(1\\) (the leading coefficient), i.e., \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 5, \\pm 10\\).\n\nLet's test \\(x = 2\\):\n\\[2^3 - 4 \\cdot 2^2 - 5 \\cdot 2 - 10 = 8 - 16 - 10 - 10 = -28 \\neq 0.\\]\nLet's test \\(x = 5\\):\n\\[5^3 - 4 \\cdot 5^2 - 5 \\cdot 5 - 10 = 125 - 100 - 25 - 10 = 0.\\]\nSo, \\(x = 5\\) is a root. We can now factor \\(x - 5\\) out of the polynomial \\(x^3 - 4x^2 - 5x - 10\\).\n\nUsing synthetic division to divide \\(x^3 - 4x^2 - 5x - 10\\) by \\(x - 5\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n5 & 1 & -4 & -5 & -10 \\\\\n  &   & 5 & 5 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe quotient is \\(x^2 + x + 2\\), so we can write:\n\\[x^3 - 4x^2 - 5x - 10 = (x - 5)(x^2 + x + 2).\\]\nThe quadratic \\(x^2 + x + 2\\) has no real roots because its discriminant is \\(1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 = 1 - 8 = -7\\), which is negative.\n\nTherefore, the only real root of the equation \\(x^3 - 4x^2 - 5x - 10 = 0\\) is \\(x = 5\\). The sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[\\boxed{5}.\\]",
