      "text": "To find the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\), we first need to simplify the right-hand side of the equation. We start by performing polynomial division of \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) by \\(x + 2\\).\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator \\(x^3\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(x^2\\).\n2. Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\).\n3. Subtract \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\) from \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n4. Divide the leading term of the new numerator \\(-4x^2\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(-4x\\).\n5. Multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n6. Subtract \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) from \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(0\\).\n\nSo, the division gives us:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} = x^2 - 4x.\n\\]\nThus, the original equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n5 = x^2 - 4x.\n\\]\nRearrange this equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\n\\]\nNext, we solve the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\\) by factoring. We look for two numbers that multiply to \\(-5\\) and add up to \\(-4\\). These numbers are \\(-5\\) and \\(1\\). Therefore, we can factor the quadratic as:\n\\[\n(x - 5)(x + 1) = 0.\n\\]\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\\[\nx - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0,\n\\]\nwhich means:\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1.\n\\]\nThe sum of the solutions to the quadratic equation is:\n\\[\n5 + (-1) = 4.\n\\]\nThus, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]",
